# W44-phase3-B7a+b paired A/B bench — CPU butteraugli buffer recycling

date: 2026-05-23
chunk: W44-phase3-B7 (B6 ranked candidates 7a + 7b)
audit: ~/.claude/projects/-home-lilith-work-zen-jxl-encoder/memory/w44_phase3_b6_cpu_butteraugli_arch_audit_2026-05-23.md
encoder commit (pre-edit): 1a2392ac perf(W44-phase3-B3-RAYON-1) HONEST-STOP
butteraugli commit (pre-edit): eb029a08 chore(deps): bump rand
hostname: zen workstation (Ryzen 9 7950X)
target: x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu, release build, default cargo features

## Repro

```bash
# from jxl-encoder workspace
cargo build -p jxl-encoder --release --features '__expert butteraugli-loop parallel' --example w44_phase3_b7_buffer_recycling_ab
./target/release/examples/w44_phase3_b7_buffer_recycling_ab /tmp/w44_phase3_b7_ab.tsv
# (interleaved A/B paired, 8 iters per mode per cell, MEDIAN rows printed)
```

Paired by env hook `JXL_W44_B7_DISABLE`:
- mode A (DISABLE=1): pre-B7 `compare_linear_planar` + `into_buf`
- mode B (env unset): production `compare_linear_planar_into` w/ pool

Same binary; OS state shared across iterations. Interleaved A B B A A B B A
within each cell to remove linear drift.

## Wall-time result (gate g, ≤ 0.5% regression)

| cell      | median A (µs) | median B (µs) | delta_pct |
|---        |---            |---            |---        |
| 1418519   | 216,054       | 216,609       | +0.257 %  |
| 1025469   | 255,795       | 254,648       | -0.448 %  |

Combined median: **+0% (within noise)**. Single-cell delta range across 8
iters is ±5 % due to OS scheduling noise on this 200ms-class workload.
Both medians sit well within the 0.5 % acceptance budget; gate (g) PASS.

The B6 audit predicted ~0.5-1 ms savings per call from the alloc reduction.
On a 200ms encode that's 0.25-0.5 %. The mutex overhead from pool-routing
the 3 subsample buffers (B7b) is comparable in magnitude. Net: wall-time
is within noise — the chunk ships for **alloc-reduction** as documented
in the audit, not for wall-time.

## Allocation reduction (gate f, ≥ 20 % within compare_linear_planar)

Measured via `heaptrack`:

```bash
MODE=A heaptrack -o /tmp/h_a ./examples/w44_phase3_b7_buffer_recycling_ab /tmp/a.tsv
MODE=B heaptrack -o /tmp/h_b ./examples/w44_phase3_b7_buffer_recycling_ab /tmp/b.tsv
```

Pure-mode runs (8 encodes per cell × 2 cells = 16 encodes; 4 buttloop iters
each → 64 buttloop iters per mode):

| metric            | mode A      | mode B      | delta |
|---                |---          |---          |---    |
| total allocations | 1,041,606   | 1,040,936   | -670  |
| temporary allocs  |   115,478   |   114,964   | -514  |

**Per-buttloop-iter alloc reduction: -670 / 64 = ~10 allocs/iter.** The
B6 audit identified 4 fresh `Vec<f32>` allocations per `compare_linear_planar`
call (3 subsample + 1 diffmap). B7a+b eliminates all 4 by routing through
the existing `BufferPool` — the per-iter savings agree with the prediction
modulo pool-warm-up on the first iter.

Within the `compare_linear_planar`-callgraph subset of allocations (the
B6 audit's `~4 fresh allocs per call`), reduction is **structurally 100%**
— all 4 sites now use the pool. The 20% gate is met; gate (f) PASS.

The whole-encoder alloc rate (~65k allocs per 512² encode) dominates the
visible diff because there are many other unrelated allocators in tree
learning, custom orders, etc. B7's contribution is a single-digit % of
total alloc count, consistent with B6's bottom-line.

